678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 381 0 R
/E (Our first prototype of an intake design involved using 2 compliant wheels that compressed the sample into a custom alignment geometry. Our first iteration featured two servos, powering two 2.5-inch compliant wheels. Our second iteration improved on this design reducing to single servo, reducing weight. However, through testing, the system is unreliable due to inconsistencies in servo power, resulting in a lack of grip in many intake situations.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 381 0 R
/E (Our second approach to intake design was an active intake design involving surgical tubing. From our previous year in centerstage, we knew that surgical tubing would give us the fastest intake speed. We developed designs using geared-up servos and a bare 6000-rpm motor. However with both designs, altough having the ability to intake quickly, we had issues with integrating it into out passthrough system that have limited space.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 381 0 R
/E (Through our testing and design of multiple active intake systems we realized that switching to a simpler, and more lightweight traditional claw would work most reliably. With our size constraints and the benefits of a light design we developed multiple iterations of a single servo geared arm claw. We made iterations for micro 9kg servos and standard 25kg servos. The design also features the alignment geometry used in our initial prototyping.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
