>>
endobj
758 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (The pivot joint is powered by 1 Axon MAX servo, and is supported with a GoBuilda 32mm ID Bearing. The extra support on the servo horn is necessary due to the force experienced by the intake claw attached to this when scoring specimens.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
759 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (The wrist joint is also powered by 1 Axon MAX servo. The power is transfered by a 1:1 gear ratio, which is used to position the servo inside the arm for a more compact arm. The overall arm uses parallel plate construction much like the rest of the robot.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
760 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 383 0 R
/T (V.1 DESIGN)
/E (V.1 DESIGN)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
761 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (The original design of this arm also had 3 degrees of freedom, but the pivot and wrist joints were powered using a belted bevel gear differential driven by two servos mounted in the arm.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
762 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (This design was chosen initially because it would allow for 2 servos worth of power in each joint due to the differential, but during prototyping, the added complexity caused major backlash issues, which prevented accurate control.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
763 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (The two servos are mounted inside the arm, and are offset to both fit inside the tight space. The power is delivered to the differential by GT2 Timing belts on a 1:1 ratio.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [32]
>>
